ORP diagnostic patch 10 — log in-clip zerofill underflow in the ALSA TX
path (the actual silent-splice mechanism).

Builds on patch 09. Code-reading AudioDeviceAlsa::writeSpaceAvailable
shows the ONE path that drops ~1 block of audio with NO snd_pcm error,
NO XRUN and NO kernel preemption: getBlocks() returns 0 mid-clip (the
single event-loop thread did not refill the source FIFO in time) and,
because zerofill_on_underflow defaults true, a block of SILENCE is
written via snd_pcm_writei() which SUCCEEDS. Patch 09 only logs the
writei()<0 / partial-write branches, so it is blind to this. Operator-
confirmed glitch (flag-20260515-182035) was exactly a ~20-24ms (~1
block) hard silent splice with all of those negatives.

Removed patch 08 logged zerofill but was pure noise (~3400 lines/day)
because it logged IDLE underflow too (benign: between transmissions the
source is legitimately empty). This patch adds the missing in-clip vs
idle discriminator: it logs ONLY when a real audio block was delivered
< 750 ms earlier, and emits one START + one END line per contiguous
splice (greppable "*** ORP-DIAG block-underflow:") with the splice
length in blocks and the inter-call gap (event-loop turn latency) so
the single-thread deadline-miss hypothesis is directly testable and
events correlate with operator-flagged audible glitches. Logging only,
no behaviour change. cerr is captured in /var/log/svxlink; <chrono> is
added for a monotonic steady_clock.

